{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggsQYEQhstR6A1FEHzTT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Answer: Dq ≈ 1650 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 810 cm⁻¹ (C = 4B ≈ 3240 cm⁻¹).** These are second-order estimates and are probably good to about ±20 cm⁻¹. I fitted only the first two bands, and I could not use the third band as a check.\n\n**1. Assign the bands**\n\nFor low-spin d⁶ with a ¹A₁g (t₂g⁶) ground state, the two lowest spin-allowed bands come from the t₂g⁵e_g¹ configuration:\n- ν₁ = 16 700 cm⁻¹ is ¹A₁g → ¹T₁g.\n- ν₂ = 26 330 cm⁻¹ is ¹A₁g → ¹T₂g.\n\n**2. First-order strong-field formulas give a poor fit**\n\nThe simple strong-field expressions are:\n- E(¹T₁g) = 10Dq − C\n- E(¹T₂g) = 10Dq + 16B − C\n\nSolving these gives B ≈ 602 and Dq ≈ 1911 cm⁻¹. These values are not reliable because they ignore configuration interaction. Here, B²/10Dq is large enough for that mixing to shift the bands by more than 3000 cm⁻¹.\n\n**3. Include configuration interaction to second order**\n\nWith C = 4B, the standard second-order expressions (as given by Lever) are:\n- ν₁ = 10Dq − 4B + 86B²/(10Dq)\n- ν₂ = 10Dq + 12B + 2B²/(10Dq)\n\nSubtracting the first from the second gives:\n- ν₂ − ν₁ = 16B − 84B²/(10Dq) = 9630 cm⁻¹\n\nI solved the two equations together by iteration:\n- B ≈ 810 cm⁻¹\n- 10Dq ≈ 16 520 cm⁻¹, so Dq ≈ 1650 cm⁻¹\n\nPutting these values back in returns ν₁ ≈ 16 690 and ν₂ ≈ 26 310 cm⁻¹, which match the observed bands.\n\n**4. The third band was not used**\n\nThe 35 020 cm⁻¹ band belongs to a t₂g⁴e_g² singlet. In the strong-field limit it sits near 20Dq. Checking it properly would need the full d⁶ Tanabe–Sugano matrices diagonalised exactly, and I did not do that here. So I have not confirmed the fit against this band.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1650, \"B\": 810, \"C\": 3240, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Fitted the first two bands with second-order strong-field expressions for low-spin d6 using C = 4B.\"}"}],"container":null,